      "Situated between eastern North America and the Caribbean to the west and western Europe and northwestern Africa to the east, the North Atlantic Ocean forms the maritime bridge uniting the historic cores of the Western world. Its oceanography is dominated by the warm Gulf Stream and the North Atlantic Drift, which carry tropical waters northeastward from the Gulf of Mexico toward Western and Northern Europe, moderating the climate of high-latitude European coasts. To the north, the basin connects with the Arctic Ocean through strategic passages flanking Greenland, Iceland, and the British Isles, while to the east the Strait of Gibraltar links it to the Mediterranean Sea.",
      "In world history, the North Atlantic was the crucible of the Columbian Exchange during the Age of Discovery, permanently reshaping the ecology, demography, and economy of both the Old and New Worlds. The sustained movement of crops, livestock, precious metals, manufactured goods, and millions of European migrants to North America transformed the basin into the central highway of the Atlantic economy. Rich fishing grounds such as the Grand Banks off Newfoundland and the shelves of Northern Europe also drew fleets across the ocean for centuries and spurred advances in naval architecture.",
      "During the industrial age and the two World Wars, the North Atlantic served as the indispensable lifeline of allied commerce and military logistics, most notably during the protracted Battle of the Atlantic against submarine warfare. It was also the site of the first transoceanic telegraph cables and now hosts the world's densest concentration of submarine fiber-optic cables linking financial hubs in New York, London, and continental Europe. The strategic unity of this maritime space ultimately gave its name to the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO).",
